{0 The checker and Quint}

The checker ([silica.check], module {!Check}) runs a component in every state
that it can reach. In each state it renders the view, finds every event that
can happen, and checks properties of the state and the view. It searches
breadth first, so a failure comes with a shortest list of events that leads
to it.

States and events must be plain data: the checker compares and hashes them.
A closure in a state or an event is reported as an [Error].

{1:component Check a component}

{!Check.component} takes a component and gives a {!Check.report}. The events
of a state are:

- the handlers of the view: press, double press, submit, focus, blur, pointer
  enter and leave, and the key handlers with each key of [keys];
- the events of the held sources of the state, such as the keys of a
  [Source.keys] table or the events of a dismiss layer;
- the events of running timers, in any order, as time is not counted;
- the answers of running calls and the states of running subscriptions (see
  {{!section-samples}Samples});
- [inputs state]: the events that need a value, such as the text of an
  [on_input];
- every value in [props], given as new props ([Check.Props p]).

A machine made with [Machine.make] has the props [()]. A machine with props
needs [props]: the check starts once with each of them. The trace of a report
is a list of {!Check.type-input}: [Check.Event e] or [Check.Props p].

A handler on an element with [disabled] is not an event. [within] limits a
state space that has no end: the checker does not explore a state outside it.

{@ocaml[
# match
    Check.component
      ~inputs:(fun _ -> [ Doc_debounce.Typed "a"; Doc_debounce.Typed "" ])
      Doc_debounce.component
  with
  | Check.Ok { states; transitions; _ } -> Printf.sprintf "%d states, %d transitions" states transitions
  | _ -> "problem";;
- : string = "3 states, 9 transitions"
]}

{1:invariants Properties}

The built-in properties hold for every component:

- Each element that can be pressed has a name that a screen reader can read.
- Refs are unique in a view, and a [Focus], [Blur] or [Scroll_into_view]
  action names a ref of the next view ([focus-target]).
- The fallback of each boundary can reset it ([boundary-reset]).
- Some event can happen, unless the state is final or the view holds a child
  component or an island ([dead-end]).
- Every running timer, call and subscription belongs to the current state
  ([timer-owner], [call-owner]).
- The machine handles every event of a handler of the view, of a timer, of
  a call answer, of a subscription and of an action result
  ([ignored-event]). It may ignore the events of held sources, of [inputs]
  and new props. So an update that ends with [| _ -> Ignore] cannot hide a
  button that does nothing.

When the machine ignores the event of a button, disable the button (for
example with {!Silica.Machine.can}), or handle the event, for example with
[Stay []]. This lamp ignores [Toggle] when it is on. The trace ends with the
ignored event:

{@ocaml[
# let broken =
    let machine =
      Silica.Machine.make ~id:"broken-lamp" ~init:Doc_lamp.Off
        ~shape:Doc_lamp.state_shape (fun state Doc_lamp.Toggle ->
          match state with
          | Doc_lamp.Off -> Silica.Machine.Go Doc_lamp.On
          | Doc_lamp.On -> Silica.Machine.Ignore)
    in
    Silica.Component.make machine Doc_lamp.view;;
val broken : (unit, Doc_lamp.state, Doc_lamp.event) Silica.Node.component =
  {Silica.Component.machine = <abstr>; view = <fun>}
# match Check.component broken with
  | Check.Violation { invariant; trace; _ } -> (invariant, trace)
  | _ -> ("none", []);;
- : string * (unit, Doc_lamp.event) Check.input list =
("ignored-event", [Check.Event Doc_lamp.Toggle; Check.Event Doc_lamp.Toggle])
]}

An invariant of the app is a name and a function of the state and the view.
This one fails on the stepper of {!page-components}, and the report gives the
shortest trace:

{@ocaml[
# let at_most_two =
    { Check.name = "at-most-two";
      holds = (fun (Doc_stepper.Count count) _ -> count <= 2) };;
val at_most_two : (Doc_stepper.state, 'a) Check.invariant =
  {Check.name = "at-most-two"; holds = <fun>}
# match
    Check.component ~props:[ "Adults" ] ~invariants:[ at_most_two ]
      ~within:(fun (Doc_stepper.Count count) -> count <= 5)
      Doc_stepper.component
  with
  | Check.Violation { invariant; trace; _ } -> (invariant, trace)
  | _ -> ("none", []);;
- : string * (string, Doc_stepper.event) Check.input list =
("at-most-two",
 [Check.Event Doc_stepper.Up; Check.Event Doc_stepper.Up;
  Check.Event Doc_stepper.Up])
]}

{!Check.pp_report} prints a report as numbered steps.

When the search ends with no violation, each name of the [states] of the
machine that the search never reached is reported in [Unreached { states }].
A name that only a test can reach goes in [allow_unreached].

[Ok] gives the result of the run: the number of [states] and [transitions],
the [edges] that the run took, the [spec] that it found (see {!section-quint}),
and [paths]: for each state name, a shortest list of steps from the start to
it.

{@ocaml[
# match Check.component Doc_lamp.component with
  | Check.Ok { paths; _ } -> paths
  | _ -> [];;
- : (string * (unit, Doc_lamp.event) Check.input list) list =
[("off", []); ("on", [Check.Event Doc_lamp.Toggle])]
]}

{1:samples Samples}

The checker has no server. A sample gives the values that the server could
give:

- {!Check.answers} [endpoint values]: a call can end with each value, and
  also with [Failed (Http 500)] and [Failed (Network "down")]. [rejected]
  gives the application errors that it can end with, as [Rejected x].
- {!Check.states} [topic values]: a subscription gets [Loading None], then up
  to [arrivals] states, one loss of the connection with the old data, and one
  fatal failure.

An endpoint with no sample can only fail. So the error paths are always
checked.

{@ocaml[
# match
    Check.component
      ~samples:[ Check.answers Tutorial_api.greeting [ "Hello, Ada" ] ]
      Doc_hello.component
  with
  | Check.Ok { states; edges; _ } -> (states, List.length edges)
  | _ -> (0, 0);;
- : int * int = (3, 3)
]}

{1:children Children}

The checker does not run a child component inside the check of its parent. A
report lists each child in [not_explored], and keeps the props that the
parent gave in [children]. Check the child alone with those props:

{@ocaml[
# let children =
    match Check.component Doc_party.component with
    | Check.Ok { not_explored; children; _ } ->
        print_endline (String.concat ", " not_explored);
        children
    | _ -> failwith "problem";;
component stepper
val children : Check.children = <abstr>
# let props = Check.props children Doc_stepper.component;;
val props : string list = ["Adults"; "Children"]
# match
    Check.component ~props
      ~within:(fun (Doc_stepper.Count count) -> count <= 3)
      Doc_stepper.component
  with
  | Check.Ok { states; _ } -> states
  | _ -> 0;;
- : int = 8
]}

{1:live Live components}

{!Check.live} checks a Live component. Its machine runs on the server, so a
state that owns a source of the page (a focus trap, a key table, a dismiss
layer) is a violation named [live-uses-client-source], and an action that
needs an answer of the page ([Measure], [Copy], [Load_script]) is a violation
named [live-uses-client-action].

{1:app Routed apps}

{!Check.app} explores the root of a router app with a simulated history: link
presses, back, forward and the navigation actions. [routes] lists route
values to check: for a route with a query, give one value with the default and
one without. [inputs] gives the URLs that a user can type. Two more
properties hold:

- [route-round-trip]: each route reads back from its own URL.
- [link-parses]: the [href] of each link in a view prints back to the same
  URL, and does not go to the not-found route.

{@ocaml[
# match
    let open Doc_books in
    Check.app
      ~inputs:(fun _ ->
        List.map
          (fun path -> Silica.Router.Url_changed (Silica.Url.of_string path))
          [ "/"; "/books/7"; "/search?q=ocaml"; "/nowhere" ])
      ~routes:
        [
          Home;
          Book 7;
          Search { text = ""; page = 1 };
          Search { text = "ocaml"; page = 2 };
          Not_found "/x";
        ]
      ~parse:(Silica.Router.parse routes) ~print:(Silica.Router.print routes)
      ~is_not_found:(Silica.Router.is_not_found routes)
      ~url_event:(fun url -> Silica.Router.Url_changed url)
      app
  with
  | Check.Ok { states; _ } -> Printf.sprintf "%d states" states
  | _ -> "problem";;
- : string = "1696 states"
]}

The pages of this example are plain nodes, so they are part of the check. A
page that is a component node is listed in [not_explored]: check it alone,
as a child.

{1:quint Diagrams and Quint}

{!Silica.Spec} is the shape of a machine that a checker run found: the states it
reached, the timers and sources of each, and the edges it took (see
{!page-machines.spec}). It is the [spec] of [Check.Ok]. It has two exports:

- {!Silica.Spec.to_dot}: a Graphviz diagram.
- {!Silica.Spec.to_quint}: a module for {{:https://quint-lang.org} Quint}, a
  language for the specification of systems. The module has the state, the
  running timers and sources, and the last event. It has one transition
  for each edge, written as a Quint [action], and the invariants
  [resources_owned] (every running resource belongs to the current state),
  [can_move] and [inv] (both).

Give the export the [edges] of the same report, and each edge gets the names of
the events that took it. The names come from the [events] of the machine (see
{!page-machines.events}); a machine with no [events] gives empty names. The
states come in a fixed order: the initial state first, then by name.

{@ocaml[
# match Check.component Doc_lamp.component with
  | Check.Ok { spec; edges; _ } ->
      print_string (Silica.Spec.to_quint ~labels:edges spec)
  | _ -> print_string "problem";;
// Generated from the spec of the machine "lamp". Do not edit.
module lamp {
  type State = | Off | On
  type Event = OnStart | OnAny | OnToggle

  var state: State
  var running: Set[str]
  var last: Event

  pure def owned(s: State): Set[str] = match s {
    | Off => Set()
    | On => Set()
  }

  pure val finals: Set[State] = Set()
  pure val movable: Set[State] = Set(Off, On)

  action enter(s: State, e: Event): bool = all {
    state' = s,
    running' = owned(s),
    last' = e,
  }

  action init = all {
    state' = Off,
    running' = owned(Off),
    last' = OnStart,
  }

  action off_to_on_on_toggle = all { state == Off, enter(On, OnToggle) }
  action on_to_off_on_toggle = all { state == On, enter(Off, OnToggle) }

  action step = any {
    off_to_on_on_toggle,
    on_to_off_on_toggle,
  }

  val resources_owned = running.subseteq(owned(state))
  val can_move = finals.contains(state) or movable.contains(state)
  val inv = resources_owned and can_move
}
- : unit = ()
]}

The model holds the edges only, not the code of the update, so it allows more
runs than the machine. It holds only the edges that the run took: give the
checker the samples and inputs that reach every state.

The repository writes the exports of its machines in a dune rule, and keeps a
copy in [spec/] next to the tests. A [diff] rule fails the build when a change
of a machine changes its spec, so a change of the shape is always seen in a
review:

From [test/example_check/dune]:
{@dune skip[
(rule
 (alias spec)
 (action
  (diff spec/router.qnt router.qnt)))
]}

[test/quint/check.ts] runs Quint on each module: it type checks it and runs
random traces against the invariant [inv]. A module with a seeded bug must
fail:

From [test/quint/check.ts]:
{@ts skip[
const runArgs = (file: string) => [
  "run", file, "--invariant=inv", "--max-steps=20", "--max-samples=500", "--seed=1", "--backend=typescript",
];
]}
